# rishi_brief_witness.rish -- the bounded capture fields, and the cliff they exist for.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/rishi_brief_witness.rish
#
# REDS %740. Composing a WHOLE capture into an `assert ... else` message drops it in SILENCE past
# 4,096 bytes -- the composed string emits the literal `${x.out}` rather than the value, a
# truncation, or a named refusal. Bisected on metal: 4,096 interpolates, 4,100 does not. The reason
# is lost exactly when there is most of it, and the failure reads like an assert that never carried
# a reason at all.
#
# `out_brief` and `err_brief` are the same captures bounded to 512 bytes and marked where they cut.
# The head is kept for `err`, since a compiler names its error first; the tail for `out`, since a
# program's last words are its reason. Both sit BESIDE the whole capture rather than replacing it.
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rishi_brief_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "the brief-capture control did not reach verdict=ok: ${control.err_brief}"
# A STALE INTERPRETER LANDS HERE, and the refusal says what to do about it. `rishi/bin/` is
# gitignored, so each ship builds its own interpreter and a pull brings these sources without the
# binary. On a ship whose rishi predates the bounded fields, `${x.err_brief}` composes to the
# LITERAL placeholder -- the same benign fallback an absent field always gives -- so nothing
# crashes and every swept refusal quietly stops carrying its reason. The control catches that,
# and this message names the cure rather than leaving a reader to find it.
assert control.out contains "verdict=ok" else "brief-capture control refused. If this ship's rishi predates the bounded capture fields, rebuild it by building rishi/src/main.rye with rye and emitting the binary to rishi/bin/rishi, then re-run. Control said: ${control.out_brief}"
assert control.out contains "faults=0" else "a planted behavior went unread"

# THE CLIFF IS ASSERTED HERE TOO, because it is the whole reason these fields exist and a tree that
# forgot it would quietly go back to composing whole captures.
assert control.out contains "leg whole_still_literal=ok" else "the 4,096-byte cliff is unproven"
assert control.out contains "leg short_err_unmarked=ok" else "a short capture was marked as cut"
assert control.out contains "leg head_keeps_first=ok" else "the err head lost its first bytes"
assert control.out contains "leg tail_keeps_last=ok" else "the out tail lost its last bytes"
assert control.out contains "leg whole_capture_kept=ok" else "the whole capture stopped being available"

say "GREEN: bounded captures say their reason, mark their cut, and leave the whole capture beside them."
